Now this type of migration where we've added, add a new column or add a new table, it tends

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:22.800
to be fairly safe, tends to be fairly safe.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:27.200
And the reason for that is the application that's been connected to this database and

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:31.160
running queries, it didn't care about this birthday field before.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:36.040
It was never say, you know, querying, give me all of the users whose birthdays are before

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:37.040
the state.

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:39.920
It wasn't making those queries by adding a new field.

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:45.140
It's very unlikely that we'll break anything by adding a new table to a database that's

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:46.220
being used.

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:51.080
Very unlikely you'll break anything because the current code simply doesn't care about

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:54.920
that database table because it just didn't exist in the last version.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:33:02.240
On the other hand, let's say we do a migration where we remove passwords from the users table.

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:06.680
Maybe we're no longer storing user passwords because we only support signed one with Google,

00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:07.960
something like that.

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:14.240
These types of migrations, right, removals of data, remove, I'll just do RM, they're

00:33:14.240 --> 00:33:15.240
very dangerous.

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:17.960
They tend to be very dangerous.

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:22.520
So let's talk through some rules of thumb about how you'd approach migrations depending

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:28.320
on if your migration is adding a resource like a column or a table, deleting a column

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:32.280
or a table, or updating because this is going to change a lot, right?

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:36.080
So let's assume that we're adding, we're adding a new column or a new table.

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.600
This tends to be safe and so what you can do first to keep things simple is first you

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:43.740
run the migration on the database.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:47.600
So you update the database, step number one, right?

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:52.400
And by updating the database, you haven't broken what's currently running because you're

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:55.600
just adding new stuff, right, again, generally speaking.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:59.980
Okay, number two, you'll update your code, right?

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:04.560
Now you can deploy the new version of the application that actually uses those new fields

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:05.560
or tables.

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:09.360
Now let's assume that you're deleting a column or a table.

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:14.560
The first thing you're actually going to do in this case is update the code, right?

00:34:14.560 --> 00:34:18.760
So presumably you're deleting a column or a table from a database because you no longer

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:21.560
need it in your application logic, right?

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:24.600
For whatever reason, you don't care about users' birthdays anymore so we're just going

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:25.600
to go ahead and delete it.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:28.900
Well, first you need to update the code.

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:31.560
Stop querying that resource, right?

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:34.680
Stop querying that column or that table.

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:40.840
Once the code has been updated, now you can safely update the database without breaking

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:41.840
anything.

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:44.200
Now let's talk about the thing that is by far the hardest.

00:34:44.200 --> 00:34:46.520
This is updating.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:50.320
Now generally speaking, you should try just to not update.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:55.400
And what I mean is changing the name of a table is generally speaking just a really

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:59.400
bad idea, changing the name of a column really bad.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:01.560
Try to get those things right the first time.

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:06.240
But if you do have to change it, just know that it's going to be more work and you need

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:07.960
to be extra careful.

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:14.100
Because keep in mind, when we're adding stuff, we can safely update the database beforehand.

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:19.220
When we're deleting stuff, we can safely update the database after the fact.

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:24.080
When we're updating stuff, there's really no safe way.

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:25.880
I mean, think about it for just a second.

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:30.720
You've got an old version of code that expects an old database name and a new version of

00:35:30.720 --> 00:35:33.880
code that expects a new database name.

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:40.080
Really your only option is to make sure that you update both the database table name and

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:44.040
the code at exactly the same moment in time.

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:46.040
And it's really hard to make sure that happens.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:49.920
You're going to have probably milliseconds or seconds of downtime.

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:54.800
So because this is a harder problem, there's not just a simple one to solution.

00:35:54.800 --> 00:36:01.680
I'm going to just give a few ideas of how you can approach updating the database.

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:04.320
Approach number one is scheduled downtime.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:05.840
Downtime.

00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:08.040
This means you literally just turn off your application.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:11.920
You're like, look, customers, I'm sorry, you cannot access the application for the next

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:12.920
30 minutes.

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:14.240
We're doing a big update to our database.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:16.800
I don't recommend that that kind of sucks, right?

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:19.440
People want access to your web application.

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:24.080
So this is like what you would do if you really feel like you don't have any other options.

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:26.520
It's simple, but it does affect your customers.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:33.680
Number two is kind of what I described at the start, which is just a same, same time

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:37.560
deploy, right?

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:40.880
So we're just going to deploy the update and we're going to deploy the code at the same

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:41.880
time.

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:46.720
We understand that there will be kind of a bug there for a few seconds.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:52.600
But if you've determined that say you don't have any active users at the moment or a few

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:57.160
seconds of issues, aren't really a problem and it's not worth doing anything more complex.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:36:59.520
I mean, I've seen companies do this.

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:03.360
I wouldn't say it's a good idea, but it does get the job done.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:11.680
The best approach or the most robust approach is probably to copy, to copy the database

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.880
or the database table, right?

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:18.980
So we create a new copy of the table with all the exact same data.

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:23.880
We can update the new copy's name to be the new table, right?

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:28.720
And now we can safely deploy the new code that references the new table.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:34.640
There is a possibility of, you know, in the interim of you've done the copy and now you've

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:38.700
deployed the new code that new records will be added to the old table that you would also

00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:41.360
need to copy, but that can also be handled, right?

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:46.640
You can write a fairly robust migration script that copies data to the new table, deploys

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:51.000
the code, makes sure it moves over any records that were added in the interim, that sort

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:52.000
of thing.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:54.160
So copying tends to be very robust.

00:37:54.160 --> 00:37:56.000
It takes a little more work, right?

00:37:56.000 --> 00:38:01.840
You're writing a more, a more complex migration scripts to make sure that everything goes

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:02.840
off without a hitch.

00:38:02.840 --> 00:38:07.520
Now, I know I just said that copying was the best approach and I may have misspoke there.

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:10.440
There actually is a better approach that I'm aware of.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:15.300
I'm going to call it aliasing.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.440
The thing is sometimes aliasing is not possible.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:23.320
So there are certain databases that support an aliasing feature and some that don't.

00:38:23.320 --> 00:38:25.340
So it kind of just depends if this is available to you.

00:38:25.340 --> 00:38:29.840
But the whole idea behind aliasing is that you can give the table a second name.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:34.520
So your database table effectively has two names it can be referenced by.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:37.560
So what that means is your old code references the old name.

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:42.240
You alias the table with a second name, so that can be referenced by two different names.

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:47.360
The new code uses the new name, right, and then once you've safely deployed the new code,

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:50.180
you can drop the old name from the table.

00:38:50.180 --> 00:38:54.080
That would actually be the best, the safest and the simplest approach, but it kind of

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.720
depends on your database technology and what's available to you.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:38:57.720
These are just four options.

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:00.800
These are the four that I've tended to use the most.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:01.800
Hopefully they help.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:06.940
And then I just want to reiterate that you shouldn't be updating names very often, right?

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:12.200
Pick good table names, pick good column names, try to do ads and deletes and avoid a bug.

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:19.240
Now, the last thing we need to talk about with database migrations are up, up and down

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:20.240
migrations.

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:21.240
Right?

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:25.480
So we talked about the different types of migrations in the sense that you can add something,

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.720
delete it, or update it.

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:34.880
But realistically, any migration that brings us forward in time, we call an up migration,

00:39:34.880 --> 00:39:35.880
right?

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:44.120
Our first up migration might create a table, right?

00:39:44.120 --> 00:39:51.600
And then our second migration might delete, I should say our second up migration might

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:55.240
delete a column from that table, right?

00:39:55.240 --> 00:40:01.120
Our third up migration might create a new table, create another table.

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:10.800
Now let's say our fourth up migration renames a column.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:16.880
And then maybe our last migration deletes, deletes a table.

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:20.640
Okay, those are all up migrations.

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:27.040
And in theory, the latest running or the current running version of our application, we'll

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:31.480
just use the latest version of the up migrations.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:37.920
In other words, it will have run every single up migration in order to arrive at kind of

00:40:37.920 --> 00:40:41.360
the final state of the database, the one that's running in production.

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:48.960
Now a down migration, a down migration is really just used to roll back changes in the

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:52.200
case of emergency or in the case that something goes wrong.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:55.280
So you don't plan to use your down migrations.

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:58.120
You just kind of have them as a backup.

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:01.720
And frankly, this is why at the very beginning of the video, I talked about how people talk

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:05.160
about great migrations, but oftentimes they don't employ them.

00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:12.080
Not every company has great plans for down migrations, just to speak frankly, it's more

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:16.480
robust to have a great down migration so that you can roll up your database and roll down

00:41:16.480 --> 00:41:19.640
your database, bring it forward and backward in time.

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:20.760
But it's a lot of work, right?

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:26.160
So you won't necessarily always see teams that have fantastic down migrations, but let's

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:27.720
just talk about what they'd look like.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:33.980
So a down migration is very simply just the opposite of its kind of related up migration.

00:41:33.980 --> 00:41:38.920
So when you add a new table, you might also log a down migration that deletes that same

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:40.480
table.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:45.640
So delete table, here we'd have add column, right?

00:41:45.640 --> 00:41:49.580
We're just doing the inverse of the equivalent up migration.

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:56.840
The third down migration would delete a table again, delete table.

00:41:56.840 --> 00:42:01.920
The fourth would rename the column back to whatever it was, right?

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:04.640
It would invert this up migration.

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:10.960
And then finally here, we would have an add table command.

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:11.960
And that's a five.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:12.960
Why did I write six?

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:15.160
There we go.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:16.160
Okay.

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:20.920
So the theory is I can go all the way up to the latest version by running these up migrations

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:21.920
in order.

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:26.640
And if I need to go back a version, I can just run the equivalent down migration, right?

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:31.920
So I get up to the latest and then I say, oh crap, I need to roll back to the last version

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:32.920
of my database.

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:39.260
I can just run this down migration number five to re-add that table that I just deleted.

00:42:39.260 --> 00:42:43.480
So as we just talked about, database migrations can be pretty dangerous.

00:42:43.480 --> 00:42:48.080
They tend to be some of the more dangerous things that you do when working with databases.

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:53.240
Like with all web development, the closer you get to the data, the more careful you

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:54.840
have to be, right?

00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:00.320
Bugs that you push to production are generally pretty easy to revert and get the right code

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:02.920
pushed out to users and get the bug fixed.

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:06.800
When you mess up data, sometimes it can be harder to revert those changes.

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:10.660
So you always have to be more careful when you're working with kind of the persisted

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:15.160
data that sticks around in your database because sometimes just changing the code back to what

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:21.040
it was won't necessarily revert the state of the file system in the database.

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:25.560
This is a pretty solid meme regarding production databases.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:27.960
I've definitely been the dog in this situation.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:31.960
You don't want to be the dev that alters the production database and brings the site down.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:34.760
It's actually, it's not fine.

00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:35.760
Moving on.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:40.160
The question associated with this first lesson about database migration says, which of the

00:43:40.160 --> 00:43:44.120
following statements about migrations is false?

00:43:44.120 --> 00:43:47.600
So first one is you can be fast and loose when writing migrations.

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:50.000
A bad migration is easy to fix.

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:51.000
That is false.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:56.960
I'm going to read the rest of them just to make sure nothing else is even worse.

00:43:56.960 --> 00:43:58.800
Well written migrations are reversible.

00:43:58.800 --> 00:43:59.800
That's generally true, right?

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:03.600
You can go up and you can go down.

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:09.920
That doesn't necessarily mean you always want to be doing that, but they are reversible.

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:14.200
A good migration takes into account any systems that rely on the existing schema.

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:15.200
That is true.

00:44:15.200 --> 00:44:19.680
You need to make sure you don't have any active code that's relying on an old version of the

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:20.920
database.

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:22.680
Migrations are incremental changes made to a database.

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:23.680
That is true.

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:24.680
Okay, cool.

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:25.680
So it is this one.

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:26.680
You should not be fast and loose with migrations.

00:44:26.680 --> 00:44:30.200
They are not easy to fix if you get them wrong.

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:35.960
The next question is will database migrations often be coupled with application code updates?

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:39.200
And that is generally speaking true, right?

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:44.840
You want to roll the database forward to a new version along with the new code that uses

00:44:44.840 --> 00:44:47.040
that new schema.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:51.640
The next question says why are good migrations written in a reversible manner?

00:44:51.640 --> 00:44:55.560
And the answers are they are not so that if something goes wrong, the changes can be rolled

00:44:55.560 --> 00:45:01.840
back or because you should always roll back changes before applying new ones.

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:03.440
Well they are.

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:07.080
Good migrations are written in a reversible manner, so it's not the first one.

00:45:07.080 --> 00:45:10.600
You don't always need to roll back changes before applying new ones.

00:45:10.600 --> 00:45:11.840
That's not how migrations work.

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:16.720
Usually you'll add one new incremental migration and just roll that one forward.

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:20.360
The middle one, so that if something goes wrong, the changes can be rolled back, that

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:22.840
is the real reason.

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:27.840
It's important to understand though that just by rolling back the schema changes doesn't

00:45:27.840 --> 00:45:31.440
necessarily mean you've fixed the data.

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:37.680
So it just kind of depends on what quote unquote went wrong, but at least when you have good

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:41.360
down migrations, you can bring the schema back to where it's supposed to be.

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:42.620
So it's going to be that middle one.

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:46.640
So we've talked about this a little bit before, but when you're writing reversible migrations,

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:51.880
the term up and down migrations really are used to refer to kind of the chronology.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:57.760
So you'll put every new change to the database schema in subsequent up migrations, right?

00:45:57.760 --> 00:46:01.880
So your first up migration might create a new table, the next one might add a column,

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:02.880
et cetera, et cetera.

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:07.080
And every down migration has one associated up migration.

00:46:07.080 --> 00:46:08.080
Okay.

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:12.280
Our assignment here where we'll be practicing with some migrations is to add additional

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:13.700
columns to the transactions table.

00:46:13.700 --> 00:46:17.580
We want to know whether or not the transaction was successfully completed between two users.

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:23.440
We also want to know, or we also want our database to track the type of transactions.

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:28.680
So our transactions table looks like this at the moment, okay?

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:32.640
Complete the up migration, complete the following up migration.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:39.000
Add the Boolean was successful column to the transactions table, and then add the text

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:41.760
transaction type column to the transactions table.

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:42.760
Okay, cool.

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:51.960
So we'll be adding two columns, alter table transactions.

00:46:51.960 --> 00:47:06.280
Add column was successful Boolean, and alter table transactions.

00:47:06.280 --> 00:47:15.600
Add column transaction type, cool.

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:21.280
Let's go ahead and take a look at that was successful Boolean transaction type text looks

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:23.520
good to me.

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:25.240
Let's talk about some data types.

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:29.760
So as I've mentioned before, we're actually using SQL lite in this course.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:34.440
And the data types that are available to you kind of do depend on the specific database

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:35.560
engine that you use it, right?

00:47:35.560 --> 00:47:37.440
So we're using SQL lite.

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:41.060
And so you'll see types like null, which means nothing's there.

00:47:41.060 --> 00:47:43.800
And actually that is pretty ubiquitous.

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:49.520
Basically every SQL database I've ever worked with supports null values.

00:47:49.520 --> 00:47:56.040
You've got integer types, which are, you know, all numbers that can also be negative, right?

00:47:56.040 --> 00:48:02.660
So negative one, negative two, zero, one, two, three, four, etc, signed integers, reels,

00:48:02.660 --> 00:48:07.400
this is your equivalent of like a floating point value or a double, depending on what

00:48:07.400 --> 00:48:12.040
programming language you're coming from, but basically numbers with a fractional part.

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:17.920
You've got text fields, and then blobs, which are basically just like big blobs of binary

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:20.080
data.

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:24.040
And then the other thing to keep in mind when working with SQL lite is just that Boolean

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:26.680
values false and true.

00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:32.360
You can use the kind of keywords false and true in your SQL, but when you get the data

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:36.680
back, it will be represented as ones and zeros.

