01:24:18.940 --> 01:24:24.800
So the auto increment keyword in SQL is a feature supported by most SQL databases that

01:24:24.800 --> 01:24:31.240
effectively allows you to automatically increment the values in a given column.

01:24:31.240 --> 01:24:34.940
So for example, this is most common with an ID column.

01:24:34.940 --> 01:24:39.320
So you have an ID column that is integer primary keys, right?

01:24:39.320 --> 01:24:46.680
So every new, say user in your database, you want them to have a new ID and you want to

01:24:46.680 --> 01:24:47.920
increment by one every time.

01:24:47.920 --> 01:24:51.880
So let's just say that you've got this user's table, it's got its ID column, and you've

01:24:51.880 --> 01:24:57.320
got four records in the database and they have IDs one, two, three, and four.

01:24:57.320 --> 01:25:01.280
What the auto increment keyword allows you to do is when you insert that fifth record,

01:25:01.280 --> 01:25:07.320
you actually don't have to specify in your SQL statement that its ID is going to be five.

01:25:07.320 --> 01:25:12.200
You can just say, hey, please auto increment this column, and the database will automatically

01:25:12.200 --> 01:25:17.560
check and say, oh, I'm already using IDs, you know, one through four.

01:25:17.560 --> 01:25:21.040
So this new record is going to have ID five.

01:25:21.040 --> 01:25:25.400
That's useful because it means when you insert a new record, you don't first have to do a

01:25:25.400 --> 01:25:31.680
database read to check which IDs are available or which ID you should use next.

