01:40:53.040 --> 01:40:55.240
Let's talk about deleting now.

01:40:55.240 --> 01:40:59.160
So when a user deletes their account on Twitter or deletes a comment on a YouTube video, that

01:40:59.160 --> 01:41:04.840
data actually needs to be removed from those respective databases, right?

01:41:04.840 --> 01:41:09.200
So when you delete your tweet, we need to actually go purge it from the database, right?

01:41:09.200 --> 01:41:11.080
The Twitter database.

01:41:11.080 --> 01:41:13.920
In SQL, there is a delete statement.

01:41:13.920 --> 01:41:14.920
Looks like this.

01:41:14.920 --> 01:41:21.000
So delete from table name, and then optionally, you can say where something equals something

01:41:21.000 --> 01:41:25.600
or you know, where something is greater than or less than or whatever, right?

01:41:25.600 --> 01:41:31.320
But the point is delete from table name is kind of the different thing.

01:41:31.320 --> 01:41:38.440
A couple of things I want to point out here, if you just do delete from employees, that

01:41:38.440 --> 01:41:42.920
will delete everything in the employees database, right?

01:41:42.920 --> 01:41:52.400
So for example, if I just do delete from users here, everything's gone, right?

01:41:52.400 --> 01:41:54.520
That's like super dangerous.

01:41:54.520 --> 01:41:59.920
So when you're working with a production database, be very, very careful with your delete statements.

01:41:59.920 --> 01:42:06.120
You do not want to delete the data when you're not intentionally trying to delete the data.

01:42:06.120 --> 01:42:15.080
The difference between delete from users and delete from users where ID equals three is

01:42:15.080 --> 01:42:16.680
a huge difference.

01:42:16.680 --> 01:42:23.840
This will delete one user, whereas if I remove that where clause, that just says delete all

01:42:23.840 --> 01:42:24.840
users.

01:42:24.840 --> 01:42:29.240
In fact, a rule of thumb that I just personally have is anytime I'm going to run a delete

01:42:29.240 --> 01:42:36.080
statement against a production database, I always run it as a select statement first,

01:42:36.080 --> 01:42:39.080
just to make sure that I got my where clause right, right?

01:42:39.080 --> 01:42:44.200
Just to make sure, and I can kind of see everything that's going to be deleted by running a select

01:42:44.200 --> 01:42:45.200
first.

01:42:45.200 --> 01:42:50.320
So again, when you're working with production data, run a select, see all of the stuff that

01:42:50.320 --> 01:42:55.960
you're going to delete, and then just change it into a delete statement and run that.

01:42:55.960 --> 01:43:00.760
Okay, the assignment says, Samantha, one of our cache pal users has opted to delete her

01:43:00.760 --> 01:43:03.520
account and stop using our app, which of course makes us sad.

01:43:03.520 --> 01:43:06.280
Anyways, we need to remove her record from the database.

01:43:06.280 --> 01:43:09.520
So delete Samantha's record from the user table.

01:43:09.520 --> 01:43:11.880
This is what our users table looks like at the moment.

01:43:11.880 --> 01:43:16.120
So it looks like Samantha has ID two.

01:43:16.120 --> 01:43:18.860
Now something to point out here.

01:43:18.860 --> 01:43:29.100
It just so happens that we could write, we could write this SQL query, delete from users

01:43:29.100 --> 01:43:36.560
where name equals some man thought, okay, we could run this.

01:43:36.560 --> 01:43:41.240
In fact, let me run it and it works, right?

01:43:41.240 --> 01:43:43.720
Samantha's gone.

01:43:43.720 --> 01:43:47.280
There's a devious little problem with this, right?

01:43:47.280 --> 01:43:54.240
It's very likely that if our app is popular, that there's many Samantha's in the database.

01:43:54.240 --> 01:43:57.780
So this is actually a really bad query to run.

01:43:57.780 --> 01:44:04.560
You could potentially delete 20 different user records, all of whom are named Samantha.

01:44:04.560 --> 01:44:09.680
So generally speaking, if you know you just want to delete one record, use the primary

01:44:09.680 --> 01:44:10.680
key.

01:44:10.680 --> 01:44:14.880
So we're going to do where ID equals two.

01:44:14.880 --> 01:44:17.040
This is much, much safer.

01:44:17.040 --> 01:44:20.480
We're guaranteed to only delete the row we care about.

01:44:20.480 --> 01:44:23.560
So I'm going to go ahead and submit it in this form.

01:44:23.560 --> 01:44:29.720
The wisest senior developers are the first to admit that their code base is a flaming

01:44:29.720 --> 01:44:36.340
pile of bug ridden garbage that has somehow survived in production as long as it has.

01:44:36.340 --> 01:44:41.240
So because you and your team are always just one bad commit away from wiping out all of

01:44:41.240 --> 01:44:45.420
your customer's data, don't you think it would be smart to back up your production databases?

01:44:45.420 --> 01:44:50.280
So let's talk about how you would actually set up database backups or how they work is

01:44:50.280 --> 01:44:52.320
probably a more accurate way to put it.

01:44:52.320 --> 01:44:57.640
So here we've got our production database, right?

01:44:57.640 --> 01:45:01.800
And every day new data is being added to the production database.

01:45:01.800 --> 01:45:03.720
And so naturally we're concerned, right?

01:45:03.720 --> 01:45:08.000
We want to make sure that even if something catastrophic happens, if this database gets

01:45:08.000 --> 01:45:11.840
deleted, if some bad code gets pushed that wipes out all of the rows in the database,

01:45:11.840 --> 01:45:14.580
right, that we have a way to restore the database.

01:45:14.580 --> 01:45:22.160
So one very common strategy is just kind of snapshot the database, let's say daily.

01:45:22.160 --> 01:45:26.680
A lot of companies I've worked at kind of just do daily snapshots.

01:45:26.680 --> 01:45:32.840
So for example, every day at midnight, an automated task runs, right, there's some code

01:45:32.840 --> 01:45:40.400
running on the server that copies all of the data out of the production database and into

01:45:40.400 --> 01:45:42.160
a snapshot for the day.

01:45:42.160 --> 01:45:52.640
So we'd say prod database copy, you know, May 1st, 2023, something like that.

01:45:52.640 --> 01:45:58.400
And that data is going to live in cold storage somewhere.

01:45:58.400 --> 01:46:00.080
It's going to be very cheap.

01:46:00.080 --> 01:46:02.720
We're going to shove it into a flat file system.

01:46:02.720 --> 01:46:07.560
We don't need to be able to access this data kind of live from our application, right?

01:46:07.560 --> 01:46:12.640
So kind of technically speaking, this might live in something like an Amazon S3 bucket

01:46:12.640 --> 01:46:20.400
or a Google cloud storage, cloud storage bucket, right, so very cheap storage, the idea being

01:46:20.400 --> 01:46:27.800
if something goes wrong, we can always kind of restore from this backup back into our

01:46:27.800 --> 01:46:29.320
prod database, right?

01:46:29.320 --> 01:46:32.480
It's not being used live, it's just it's just a backup.

01:46:32.480 --> 01:46:38.600
And then of course, the next day we would take, let me change colors back.

01:46:38.600 --> 01:46:52.360
The next day we would take a new snapshot and name it prod database copy, May 2nd, 2023,

01:46:52.360 --> 01:46:53.360
right?

01:46:53.360 --> 01:46:54.360
You get the idea.

01:46:54.360 --> 01:46:56.360
We're taking snapshots daily.

01:46:56.360 --> 01:47:01.120
And then usually what would happen is we'd only keep the snapshots for a certain amount

01:47:01.120 --> 01:47:02.120
of time, right?

01:47:02.120 --> 01:47:06.360
We might take a snapshot every day, but only keep them around for a month.

01:47:06.360 --> 01:47:10.920
So once we have a month's worth of snapshots, we start deleting the old snapshots, just

01:47:10.920 --> 01:47:15.560
so we don't need to store all of that data forever, because that could start to get expensive.

01:47:15.560 --> 01:47:17.880
Now you may have already noticed there's a problem here, right?

01:47:17.880 --> 01:47:23.040
If we're just taking a snapshot every day, that means that we take our snapshot, more

01:47:23.040 --> 01:47:26.820
data is added to the database, and then something bad happens.

01:47:26.820 --> 01:47:31.800
If we restore from that last snapshot, we're actually going to lose whatever happened in

01:47:31.800 --> 01:47:35.800
the last, you know, five or six hours since that snapshot was taken.

01:47:35.800 --> 01:47:41.360
So like the simple way to solve this or to mitigate this would be to, instead of doing

01:47:41.360 --> 01:47:44.460
daily snapshots, you could do hourly snapshots, right?

01:47:44.460 --> 01:47:47.480
You still have the same problem, but now you have it at a smaller scale.

01:47:47.480 --> 01:47:51.160
You'll never lose more than say 59 minutes worth of data.

01:47:51.160 --> 01:47:56.880
Now it's worth pointing out that for most small companies, this snapshot at a, you know,

01:47:56.880 --> 01:48:01.780
hourly or kind of daily rate, um, is fine, right?

01:48:01.780 --> 01:48:05.380
You hopefully don't lose your production database anyways, but if you do, you're only going

01:48:05.380 --> 01:48:07.000
to lose a couple hours of data.

01:48:07.000 --> 01:48:08.800
It's probably not mission critical.

01:48:08.800 --> 01:48:12.240
Your business probably won't go out of business, right?

01:48:12.240 --> 01:48:15.260
And this is a very simple thing to set up, right?

01:48:15.260 --> 01:48:21.200
Most cloud providers of databases, things like Amazon RDS or Google Cloud SQL have literally

01:48:21.200 --> 01:48:24.580
just a button you can click to set up automated snapshots.

01:48:24.580 --> 01:48:28.520
So if you're working at a small company or on a small project, snapshots are probably

01:48:28.520 --> 01:48:29.520
the way to go.

01:48:29.520 --> 01:48:31.420
Let's talk about kind of a more robust option.

01:48:31.420 --> 01:48:34.240
Say you can't afford to lose any data.

01:48:34.240 --> 01:48:39.800
What you might do in that case is, I mean, you'd still have your, your prod.

01:48:39.800 --> 01:48:42.920
Let me, let me make this a little bit bigger.

01:48:42.920 --> 01:48:48.040
You'd still have your prod database, but now instead of taking hourly or daily snapshots,

01:48:48.040 --> 01:48:55.840
what you might do is anytime a change is enacted on the prod database, you might set up a system

01:48:55.840 --> 01:49:07.600
where that change is copied to some, to some append only log, append only log, right?

01:49:07.600 --> 01:49:17.120
So for example, if you create a record here, you would also in the append only log, log

01:49:17.120 --> 01:49:20.560
the fact that you created the record.

01:49:20.560 --> 01:49:28.080
Similarly, if you then delete a record, keep in mind, when you delete a record in the prod

01:49:28.080 --> 01:49:30.920
database, it's just gone, poof, right?

01:49:30.920 --> 01:49:35.440
But in the append only log, you would just log that you deleted a record.

01:49:35.440 --> 01:49:40.400
So log that record was deleted.

01:49:40.400 --> 01:49:49.340
The difference is in the append only log, right, we still have, we still have the log

01:49:49.340 --> 01:49:50.840
of the record being created.

01:49:50.840 --> 01:49:56.800
So we could kind of go back and replay that record creation event if we need to.

01:49:56.800 --> 01:50:01.840
So this kind of a system is frankly a bit more complex to set up, a bit more expensive

01:50:01.840 --> 01:50:07.920
to set up, but can give us a little more granularity and robustness in the sense that we won't

01:50:07.920 --> 01:50:10.760
ever lose any real time data.

01:50:10.760 --> 01:50:15.080
And I quickly just want to reiterate the fact that for most companies, especially smaller

01:50:15.080 --> 01:50:20.280
companies, the snapshot update works just fine, and you only kind of venture into this

01:50:20.280 --> 01:50:22.620
more complex set up if you really needed to.

01:50:22.620 --> 01:50:27.000
So we already touched on this briefly, but deleting data can be really, really dangerous,

01:50:27.000 --> 01:50:28.000
right?

01:50:28.000 --> 01:50:31.440
If you delete stuff that you didn't want to delete, you'll be sad, right?

01:50:31.440 --> 01:50:37.040
Especially you don't have any sort of backup strategy, you know, set up.

01:50:37.040 --> 01:50:42.940
So we've already talked a little bit about some of the different backup strategies.

01:50:42.940 --> 01:50:47.060
Just know that there's really two high level things that you want to think about.

01:50:47.060 --> 01:50:52.480
The first is, one, make sure that you have a backup strategy for any production database,

01:50:52.480 --> 01:50:53.480
right?

01:50:53.480 --> 01:50:56.800
Make sure that you're at least taking snapshots at regular intervals, right?

01:50:56.800 --> 01:51:02.080
So that, you know, if something goes catastrophically wrong today, you can at least restore the

01:51:02.080 --> 01:51:04.000
database to what it was yesterday.

01:51:04.000 --> 01:51:08.260
Yes, some people will lose their data, and that really sucks, but it's not as bad as

01:51:08.260 --> 01:51:12.160
losing all of the data in the application for all time.

01:51:12.160 --> 01:51:15.280
So definitely have some sort of backup strategy.

01:51:15.280 --> 01:51:21.900
Number two is sometimes you will want to do soft deletes.

01:51:21.900 --> 01:51:29.320
And a soft delete is just where instead of taking a snapshot of the entire database,

01:51:29.320 --> 01:51:32.160
you just never delete stuff from the database.

01:51:32.160 --> 01:51:38.880
You never run a SQL delete statement, and instead you just mark records as deleted,

01:51:38.880 --> 01:51:39.880
right?

01:51:39.880 --> 01:51:45.080
You have like a deleted at column on your database table, right?

01:51:45.080 --> 01:51:49.120
And when something is deleted, you'd like set that timestamp to the time that it was

01:51:49.120 --> 01:51:50.120
deleted at.

01:51:50.120 --> 01:51:55.300
And then later when you select data out in your application logic, you just like ignore

01:51:55.300 --> 01:51:57.600
stuff that's been marked as deleted.

01:51:57.600 --> 01:52:01.980
So this makes it so you never actually delete anything.

01:52:01.980 --> 01:52:02.980
Applications do this.

01:52:02.980 --> 01:52:05.920
The only time you really need to be wary of doing this, I mean, there's actually two things.

01:52:05.920 --> 01:52:11.360
The first is it adds a lot of complexity to your application, because now you have to

01:52:11.360 --> 01:52:15.680
actually like be filtering based on this deleted at flag.

01:52:15.680 --> 01:52:18.500
So it actually is more work to do it this way.

01:52:18.500 --> 01:52:23.180
The other problem is you potentially have like GDPR or privacy concerns, right?

01:52:23.180 --> 01:52:28.240
Because now when your users delete their accounts, their data is not actually being deleted from

01:52:28.240 --> 01:52:32.260
your database, which again, like from kind of an ethical privacy standpoint might not

01:52:32.260 --> 01:52:33.260
be what you want.

01:52:33.260 --> 01:52:34.760
So just some things to keep in mind.

01:52:34.760 --> 01:52:41.640
Okay, the question says, you should have or you should blank have automated backups being

01:52:41.640 --> 01:52:45.360
taken of a production database, answers are sometimes never almost never or almost always,

01:52:45.360 --> 01:52:49.760
it's going to be almost always like please, please have backups.

01:52:49.760 --> 01:52:54.480
Next question says, a soft delete is where you blank, delete some data, but it's not

01:52:54.480 --> 01:52:59.440
actually removed from the database for 30 days, delete data from a snapshot, mark a

01:52:59.440 --> 01:53:04.160
row as deleted instead of actually removing the data, or delete some data by asking your

01:53:04.160 --> 01:53:09.720
database in a very nice soothing voice, usually with a please.

01:53:09.720 --> 01:53:14.120
It's going to be marking a row as deleted.

01:53:14.120 --> 01:53:18.400
Soft delete, mark it as deleted, don't actually remove the data.

01:53:18.400 --> 01:53:23.160
One last thing I forgot to say about soft deletes is just like it can potentially be

01:53:23.160 --> 01:53:29.360
expensive in the sense that like, if you have a very large database table and say like 90%

01:53:29.360 --> 01:53:33.920
of the data is deleted, if you're keeping it around and just having it marked as deleted,

01:53:33.920 --> 01:53:36.720
your queries might start to get slow, even though you've deleted a lot of data.

01:53:36.720 --> 01:53:42.480
So you know, soft deleting will never like make your database faster due to like decreasing

01:53:42.480 --> 01:53:43.840
the amount of data in it.

01:53:43.840 --> 01:53:47.080
So just of course, just another thing to keep in mind.

01:53:47.080 --> 01:53:49.080
Let's talk about updating data.

