01:55:51.160 --> 01:55:56.960
I want to briefly touch on the idea of an ORM object relational mapping.

01:55:56.960 --> 01:56:00.920
If you're not new to web development, you'll almost certainly have heard of ORMs or maybe

01:56:00.920 --> 01:56:06.440
even used one before, basically the, an ORM is just a library that makes it much easier

01:56:06.440 --> 01:56:09.120
to interact with a database.

01:56:09.120 --> 01:56:15.360
And even more specifically, the whole idea of object relational mapping is to map kind

01:56:15.360 --> 01:56:23.680
of our in-memory representation of some object or some struct or some record, map it to the

01:56:23.680 --> 01:56:28.560
database schema that we're using, typically the table, right?

01:56:28.560 --> 01:56:36.400
So for example, we might have in Golang a struct called user, and a user has an ID,

01:56:36.400 --> 01:56:40.880
which is an integer, a name, which is a string, and is admin, which is a Boolean, right?

01:56:40.880 --> 01:56:44.320
And this is Golang code, right?

01:56:44.320 --> 01:56:49.280
If we're using an ORM, then what we might be able to do is just create an instance of

01:56:49.280 --> 01:56:50.280
a user, right?

01:56:50.280 --> 01:56:58.280
ID 10, name, lane, is admin false, and use some sort of method or function like database.create,

01:56:58.280 --> 01:57:05.280
give it the user, and it will just kind of automatically generate the SQL that will run

01:57:05.280 --> 01:57:06.480
that insert statement, right?

01:57:06.480 --> 01:57:10.080
So generate an insert statement and execute it against the database.

01:57:10.080 --> 01:57:15.320
This is really convenient, right, because it means we don't have to worry about writing

01:57:15.320 --> 01:57:21.200
all of the raw SQL, we can kind of automatically map objects in our programming language to

01:57:21.200 --> 01:57:24.440
rows in our databases.

01:57:24.440 --> 01:57:26.760
There are good and bad things about ORMs.

01:57:26.760 --> 01:57:33.320
I would argue that generally speaking, you'll want to use an ORM, as long as the ORM makes

01:57:33.320 --> 01:57:38.520
it easy to also write straight SQL, right?

01:57:38.520 --> 01:57:44.320
So you still get this escape hatch where you can do very specific and very, I don't want

01:57:44.320 --> 01:57:48.920
to say complex, but more advanced things with your SQL, right?

01:57:48.920 --> 01:57:54.880
Sometimes very simple functions like create, update, delete, will get you really far, but

01:57:54.880 --> 01:57:57.320
later you'll need to write more complex queries, right?

01:57:57.320 --> 01:57:59.440
Things that we'll cover later in this course.

01:57:59.440 --> 01:58:04.480
And if your ORM is limiting you from using the full flexibility or the full power of

01:58:04.480 --> 01:58:08.360
SQL as a language, then that can be a disadvantage.

01:58:08.360 --> 01:58:18.040
So to like sum up and give you a rule of thumb, an ORM typically trades simplicity for control.

01:58:18.040 --> 01:58:22.960
And again, in production, what that often means is you will use an ORM for most stuff,

01:58:22.960 --> 01:58:27.280
and then occasionally you'll need to write some raw SQL when you're trying to do something

01:58:27.280 --> 01:58:28.880
very, very specific.

01:58:28.880 --> 01:58:29.880
Cool.

01:58:29.880 --> 01:58:35.920
The question here is, when using an ORM, you blank, call methods and functions made available

01:58:35.920 --> 01:58:41.120
via the ORMs API, or you write a lot of raw SQL.

01:58:41.120 --> 01:58:46.680
And the answer is you call methods and functions made available from the ORMs API.

01:58:46.680 --> 01:58:51.480
Next question says, one advantage of an ORM is that it blank, gives you more control over

01:58:51.480 --> 01:58:56.400
your database, is easier to debug at a low level, makes your code less verbose, or ensures

01:58:56.400 --> 01:58:58.200
faster queries.

01:58:58.200 --> 01:59:00.600
This isn't actually, this is actually interesting.

01:59:00.600 --> 01:59:03.380
The answer is that it makes your code less verbose, right?

01:59:03.380 --> 01:59:08.880
You don't have to write nearly as much code when you're using an ORM, you just call some

01:59:08.880 --> 01:59:14.080
function or some method that's been rebuilt for you by the library.

01:59:14.080 --> 01:59:15.600
So it does make your code less verbose.

01:59:15.600 --> 01:59:20.120
However, I do want to point out that it does not give you more control over your database.

01:59:20.120 --> 01:59:22.760
In some cases, it actually takes control away.

01:59:22.760 --> 01:59:27.600
It's also not easier to debug at a low level, because if you want to figure out what SQL

01:59:27.600 --> 01:59:31.920
is being run against your database from this method, you're going to have to do some digging,

01:59:31.920 --> 01:59:32.920
right?

01:59:32.920 --> 01:59:37.160
So you're just looking at your code and, you know, fixing your SQL.

01:59:37.160 --> 01:59:42.680
Also ensures faster queries, again, not necessarily the case, sometimes, especially when your

01:59:42.680 --> 01:59:48.520
ORM code starts to get a little hairy, it can really generate inefficient SQL, which

01:59:48.520 --> 01:59:50.040
again can be hard to debug.

01:59:50.040 --> 01:59:55.160
So there's trade offs with ORMs, I'm definitely not going to say they're bad or they're good,

01:59:55.160 --> 01:59:56.360
use as intended.

01:59:56.360 --> 01:59:58.800
So should you use an ORM?

01:59:58.800 --> 02:00:03.920
Almost always, it depends on the project slash team, almost never, always.

02:00:03.920 --> 02:00:08.040
I'm going to go with it depends on the project slash team.

02:00:08.040 --> 02:00:12.600
There are instances where an ORM makes a ton of sense and there are instances where it

02:00:12.600 --> 02:00:13.600
doesn't.

02:00:13.600 --> 02:00:18.280
Moving on, let's talk about aliases.

02:00:18.280 --> 02:00:26.200
So there is an AS keyword, AS AS, in SQL that allows us to essentially rename different

02:00:26.200 --> 02:00:31.920
words in our SQL queries, and we would typically do this to make the query a little bit more

02:00:31.920 --> 02:00:35.400
concise, easier to type, less verbose.

02:00:35.400 --> 02:00:40.120
And it's important to understand that these aliases, they only last for the duration

02:00:40.120 --> 02:00:41.120
of the query.

02:00:41.120 --> 02:00:46.960
So really the convenience they provide is just for kind of within the SQL itself.

02:00:46.960 --> 02:00:50.520
It doesn't persist, it doesn't get saved to the database or anything like that.

02:00:50.520 --> 02:00:53.320
It's really just to make writing the query itself a little simpler.

02:00:53.320 --> 02:00:54.760
Let me show you an example.

02:00:54.760 --> 02:01:01.880
So let's pretend that we have two columns, employee ID and employee name on an employee's

02:01:01.880 --> 02:01:02.880
table.

02:01:02.880 --> 02:01:06.680
Now, first of all, I would argue that this naming scheme isn't very good.

02:01:06.680 --> 02:01:11.800
I would have preferred to just see an ID column and a name column, because remember these

02:01:11.800 --> 02:01:17.240
columns are on the ID's table, so it's pretty obvious that it's an employee ID and an employee

02:01:17.240 --> 02:01:18.240
name.

02:01:18.240 --> 02:01:22.240
So you kind of have some redundancy here in the column names.

02:01:22.240 --> 02:01:25.660
But either way, let's just assume that this is the schema we're working with.

02:01:25.660 --> 02:01:34.220
What we can do is alias the employee ID column as just ID and the employee name column as

02:01:34.220 --> 02:01:36.180
just name.

02:01:36.180 --> 02:01:37.180
That does two things.

02:01:37.180 --> 02:01:42.760
It means that later in the query, we can refer to those columns as just ID and name, right?

02:01:42.760 --> 02:01:44.800
So it makes writing the SQL a little simpler.

02:01:44.800 --> 02:01:49.760
It also means that when the data comes back from the database, like the literal rows that

02:01:49.760 --> 02:01:56.160
we get back from the database, the keys for those columns will now be ID and name instead

02:01:56.160 --> 02:01:58.400
of employee ID and employee name.

02:01:58.400 --> 02:02:03.920
So kind of useful from a structuring the return data standpoint as well.

02:02:03.920 --> 02:02:06.560
So let's see what this looks like in an actual assignment.

