02:03:44.600 --> 02:03:47.120
Let's talk about functions in SQL.

02:03:47.120 --> 02:03:53.960
So remember that SQL is a language and like most programming language, it supports functions.

02:03:53.960 --> 02:03:59.240
So in SQL, functions look very similar to functions that you've used in other programming

02:03:59.240 --> 02:04:00.240
languages.

02:04:00.240 --> 02:04:05.200
And when you call them, you use parentheses and you pass in some number of arguments,

02:04:05.200 --> 02:04:06.200
right?

02:04:06.200 --> 02:04:12.280
So in this case, we've got the IIF function, open parentheses to start passing in parameters,

02:04:12.280 --> 02:04:17.440
and then we're passing in 123 parameters.

02:04:17.440 --> 02:04:19.640
And then we're closing the function call.

02:04:19.640 --> 02:04:22.880
Now you might be wondering what the IIF function does.

02:04:22.880 --> 02:04:29.200
Well, the IIF function is essentially a ternary, if you're familiar with ternaries from JavaScript.

02:04:29.200 --> 02:04:34.460
And basically what it says is, first, give me a condition.

02:04:34.460 --> 02:04:40.800
If the condition is true, I will return the first value you give me.

02:04:40.800 --> 02:04:47.600
But if the if the condition is false, I'll return the second value, right?

02:04:47.600 --> 02:04:51.320
And actually, if you look closely, there's actually a slight bug here, because if these

02:04:51.320 --> 02:04:55.840
two values are the same, then it will return the string car B is bigger.

02:04:55.840 --> 02:05:02.640
So this would probably say car B is greater than or equal to something like that.

02:05:02.640 --> 02:05:04.040
Anyways, irrelevance.

02:05:04.040 --> 02:05:06.840
The point is, you get how this works, right?

02:05:06.840 --> 02:05:09.680
Conditions true, we return the first thing conditions false, we return the second thing.

02:05:09.680 --> 02:05:16.920
So IIF can be really useful if we're trying to like compute something based on some conditional.

02:05:16.920 --> 02:05:18.760
So when would I use this kind of a function?

02:05:18.760 --> 02:05:21.240
Well, let's take a look at the example.

02:05:21.240 --> 02:05:29.040
Let's say we have a product table, and we want to return kind of how many we have of

02:05:29.040 --> 02:05:30.040
each product, right?

02:05:30.040 --> 02:05:33.960
So say we've got, I don't know, 10 apples and 30 bananas in stock, right?

02:05:33.960 --> 02:05:37.520
We want to return the the sheer amount that we have.

02:05:37.520 --> 02:05:44.200
But we also want to return a little text string that that's a little more descriptive, right,

02:05:44.200 --> 02:05:47.000
that can be computed from the quantity itself.

02:05:47.000 --> 02:05:52.160
So in this case, we're saying if the quantity is less than 10, we're also going to return

02:05:52.160 --> 02:05:59.400
the string or we're also going to put in the return row, the string order more.

02:05:59.400 --> 02:06:05.960
Otherwise, if we have at least 10, we're going to put the string in stock in the row.

02:06:05.960 --> 02:06:08.440
And you can see why this would be really useful, right?

02:06:08.440 --> 02:06:13.880
Now you can give your employee this report, and it will just clearly state on every row,

02:06:13.880 --> 02:06:14.880
right?

02:06:14.880 --> 02:06:19.160
9, order more, right, bananas, 11, in stock.

02:06:19.160 --> 02:06:23.560
Now they can just take a look at the order more or the in stock column to see whether

02:06:23.560 --> 02:06:26.760
or not they should order more of whatever it is, right?

02:06:26.760 --> 02:06:33.720
We can do interesting kind of computed columns in our return data by using a function like

02:06:33.720 --> 02:06:34.720
this.

02:06:34.720 --> 02:06:40.720
And you can see we're actually coupling this with an as clause so that when the data comes

02:06:40.720 --> 02:06:44.820
back, it has a nice kind of column name to it.

