02:17:28.800 --> 02:17:33.240
But basically, the in operator returns true or false.

02:17:33.240 --> 02:17:38.000
If the first operand matches any of the values in the second operand.

02:17:38.000 --> 02:17:42.640
So it's kind of a shorthand for like multiple or conditions.

02:17:42.640 --> 02:17:46.960
So take a look at this, we've got this first query, select product name and shipment status

02:17:46.960 --> 02:17:53.980
from the products table, where shipment status is in this kind of list of options.

02:17:53.980 --> 02:17:59.120
So as long as shipment status is one of these shipping, preparing or out of stock, it will

02:17:59.120 --> 02:18:03.000
return true, which means we'll return the row, right?

02:18:03.000 --> 02:18:07.720
Whereas here, we've got select product name, shipment status from products, where shipment

02:18:07.720 --> 02:18:12.520
status is shipped or shipment status is preparing or shipment status is out of stock.

02:18:12.520 --> 02:18:15.520
These are identical queries, they do the exact same thing.

02:18:15.520 --> 02:18:18.980
This is just kind of a more convenient way to write it.

02:18:18.980 --> 02:18:26.720
And later, we'll see how this allows us to kind of use the in operator with dynamic data.

02:18:26.720 --> 02:18:31.840
So we don't have to like explicitly spell out all of the different options, right?

02:18:31.840 --> 02:18:37.400
Maybe we're comparing against other data that's somewhere else in the database, cool.

