It's kind of the first operator that we've used that allows for like fuzzy matching,

02:19:41.840 --> 02:19:42.840
right?

02:19:42.840 --> 02:19:45.280
Or kind of wild card matching.

02:19:45.280 --> 02:19:50.120
So the first thing to understand is that the like keyword is going to allow us to look

02:19:50.120 --> 02:19:55.040
stuff up in the database by like partial matches, right?

02:19:55.040 --> 02:19:58.640
So we'll get to that.

02:19:58.640 --> 02:20:00.840
It'll make more sense with an example.

02:20:00.840 --> 02:20:03.800
First let me explain this kind of percent operator.

02:20:03.800 --> 02:20:09.560
So this percent sign operator will match to zero or more characters, and we can use this

02:20:09.560 --> 02:20:17.320
operator within the query string that's supplied to the like clause to find more than just

02:20:17.320 --> 02:20:19.700
exact matches depending on where we place it.

02:20:19.700 --> 02:20:27.840
So for example, select star from products where product name is like this string, banana

02:20:27.840 --> 02:20:28.840
percent.

02:20:28.840 --> 02:20:37.240
And what we're saying is any product names that just start with the string banana, we

02:20:37.240 --> 02:20:38.240
want to match, right?

02:20:38.240 --> 02:20:44.200
Because this this percent sign effectively says anything else can be here, right?

02:20:44.200 --> 02:20:50.900
Zero or more characters can be in this spot in the string.

02:20:50.900 --> 02:20:56.320
So similarly, if we want every product name that ends in banana, we just put the percent

02:20:56.320 --> 02:20:58.720
at the beginning, right?

02:20:58.720 --> 02:21:04.040
And then if we just want to do a contains, right, does the string banana show up anywhere

02:21:04.040 --> 02:21:09.440
in the product name, then we can just slap a percent sign on both sides of the string.

