And I want to like get all of the users, but first I want to sort them by like how many

02:29:42.000 --> 02:29:44.000
exercises they've completed, right?

02:29:44.000 --> 02:29:45.360
And then just give me the top 10.

02:29:45.360 --> 02:29:49.560
Like that's a very common type of query that you're going to make in a database.

02:29:49.560 --> 02:29:51.240
So let's take a look at an example.

02:29:51.240 --> 02:29:56.360
So here we've got, you know, select name, price, quantity from products, order by price.

02:29:56.360 --> 02:29:57.360
Okay.

02:29:57.360 --> 02:30:00.860
So this is going to sort by the price field.

02:30:00.860 --> 02:30:06.720
Now by default, it's going to sort in ascending order, right?

02:30:06.720 --> 02:30:13.360
In other words, it's going to put the lowest values at the top and go down from there,

02:30:13.360 --> 02:30:14.600
right?

02:30:14.600 --> 02:30:20.940
Whereas, or sorry, it's going to put the lowest values at the top and then increase in value

02:30:20.940 --> 02:30:25.360
as you go down row by row, that's ascending order, right?

02:30:25.360 --> 02:30:29.840
If you want descending order, you have to explicitly specify it, right?

02:30:29.840 --> 02:30:34.560
So order by quantity descending, right?

02:30:34.560 --> 02:30:38.520
And that would, again, that would give us the highest values at the top.

02:30:38.520 --> 02:30:41.920
And then the values would drop as we go down.

