02:58:48.960 --> 02:58:51.160
Next we've got a little quiz.

02:58:51.160 --> 02:58:58.240
So it says a sub query, a creates a temporary table, B allows you to query the result set

02:58:58.240 --> 02:59:02.680
of a nested query, or C is a technique for speeding up your database.

02:59:02.680 --> 02:59:08.680
Now it doesn't create a temporary table, a sub query only exists at execution time.

02:59:08.680 --> 02:59:13.600
So no data is actually stored in the database, like the result set of your sub query is not

02:59:13.600 --> 02:59:17.040
stored in a new table in the database or a temporary table in the database.

02:59:17.040 --> 02:59:19.040
That's not what's happening.

02:59:19.040 --> 02:59:24.520
It's just a way to express a subset or a result set within your query.

02:59:24.520 --> 02:59:27.760
It only exists at execution time.

02:59:27.760 --> 02:59:30.800
And it's not a technique for speeding up your database.

02:59:30.800 --> 02:59:35.600
It's not any sort of like performance optimization, at least not inherently.

02:59:35.600 --> 02:59:39.320
So the answer is it allows you to query the result set of a nested query.

