03:03:33.120 --> 03:03:38.000
Another quiz, SQL can only operate on data stored in tables.

03:03:38.000 --> 03:03:43.880
That is false, as we just saw.

03:03:43.880 --> 03:03:46.040
Architecting a database can be really tricky.

03:03:46.040 --> 03:03:50.680
But the most important thing to get right is that you've modeled your one to one relationships,

03:03:50.680 --> 03:03:54.320
your one to many and your many to many relationships accurately.

03:03:54.320 --> 03:03:57.320
If you do that, everything else will start to fall into place.

03:03:57.320 --> 03:03:58.320
Let's talk about.

03:03:58.320 --> 03:04:02.400
There are really three different types of relationships that we can have between entities

03:04:02.400 --> 03:04:04.600
in our database, right?

03:04:04.600 --> 03:04:12.040
We've got one to one, one to many, and many to many.

03:04:12.040 --> 03:04:17.040
And to draw that out, look something like this.

03:04:17.040 --> 03:04:23.760
If I have one of entity number one, it relates to one thing of entity number two.

03:04:23.760 --> 03:04:28.040
Another thing of entity number one, it relates to exactly one other thing of entity number

03:04:28.040 --> 03:04:31.680
two, you get the idea.

03:04:31.680 --> 03:04:33.920
One to many would look more like this.

03:04:33.920 --> 03:04:39.360
We have some things of type number one or of entity number one, and they might relate

03:04:39.360 --> 03:04:47.080
to three different types of entity number two.

03:04:47.080 --> 03:04:50.240
The second one might relate to two.

03:04:50.240 --> 03:04:54.000
This third one might just relate to one.

03:04:54.000 --> 03:04:57.440
It's really one to zero to many.

03:04:57.440 --> 03:05:00.760
You might even have one that doesn't relate to anything there.

03:05:00.760 --> 03:05:01.760
Okay, cool.

03:05:01.760 --> 03:05:04.520
And then a many to many relationship is exactly what it sounds like.

03:05:04.520 --> 03:05:10.480
We'll have all these different instances of thing number one, some instances of thing

03:05:10.480 --> 03:05:21.680
number two, and we can have kind of multiple relationships going on at the same time.

03:05:21.680 --> 03:05:23.720
Now let's start simple.

03:05:23.720 --> 03:05:29.320
Let's say we have in our database, a users table.

03:05:29.320 --> 03:05:34.040
Actually let's go with students, a students table, be a little bit more specific.

03:05:34.040 --> 03:05:36.680
And in the students table, we have two fields.

03:05:36.680 --> 03:05:41.960
A student has an ID, and a student has a name.

03:05:41.960 --> 03:05:46.960
Let's say we are thinking about adding a new entity to our database, and this entity is

03:05:46.960 --> 03:05:53.400
a GPA, right, a grade point average, and we're trying to decide how we're going to relate

03:05:53.400 --> 03:05:55.240
a GPA to a student.

03:05:55.240 --> 03:06:01.640
Well, once we decide that it's a one to one relationship, meaning a GPA and a student

03:06:01.640 --> 03:06:08.520
are tightly coupled to one another, right, a student has one and only one GPA, and that

03:06:08.520 --> 03:06:12.920
GPA is related to one and only one student, right.

03:06:12.920 --> 03:06:16.660
If that's the case, then it's pretty simple.

03:06:16.660 --> 03:06:22.560
We just add a new field, right, we just add a new field called GPA.

03:06:22.560 --> 03:06:27.680
Generally speaking, with a one to one relationship, it's easiest to model that as just another

03:06:27.680 --> 03:06:30.380
field on a database table.

03:06:30.380 --> 03:06:31.960
There is one other option though.

03:06:31.960 --> 03:06:36.320
What we could do, and I would argue this is typically a little less common because it's

03:06:36.320 --> 03:06:40.640
a little more complex, but I've seen it around, is that we'd create a new table, and we might

03:06:40.640 --> 03:06:51.400
call it something like, I don't know, student credentials, or maybe student scores.

03:06:51.400 --> 03:06:57.720
In this second table, we would have essentially the same ID, and the reason we'd have the

03:06:57.720 --> 03:07:05.720
same ID is to make sure that this relationship remains one to one.

03:07:05.720 --> 03:07:09.120
At the end of the day, we want to make sure that both of these tables have the exact same

03:07:09.120 --> 03:07:15.000
number of records, so that one record in one table will relate to exactly one record in

03:07:15.000 --> 03:07:16.120
the other table.

03:07:16.120 --> 03:07:20.840
We would typically do this for organizational or performance reasons, right.

03:07:20.840 --> 03:07:28.240
It's that we want to take this field here, the GPA field, and move it into a new table

03:07:28.240 --> 03:07:34.600
and kind of keep it separated physically and logically from the students table, right.

03:07:34.600 --> 03:07:35.960
It would give us some flexibility.

03:07:35.960 --> 03:07:43.320
Maybe we have some other data in here, like a test score that we'd want to add as another

03:07:43.320 --> 03:07:44.680
field on this table.

03:07:44.680 --> 03:07:49.920
So one to one relationships, generally speaking, it's just a field on the same database table,

03:07:49.920 --> 03:07:56.400
but it might be a kind of one to one mapping of primary keys between two tables.

03:07:56.400 --> 03:07:58.540
Now let's talk about one to many.

03:07:58.540 --> 03:08:03.600
Now one to many tends to be the most common type of relationship that you'll see in web

03:08:03.600 --> 03:08:05.160
development, right.

03:08:05.160 --> 03:08:07.240
Imagine a user and their tweets, right.

03:08:07.240 --> 03:08:13.320
You have one user and that one user can have zero to many different tweets that they've

03:08:13.320 --> 03:08:14.320
authored.

03:08:14.320 --> 03:08:15.320
Let's go ahead and model this out.

03:08:15.320 --> 03:08:25.600
So let's say that users have an ID and a name, seems pretty straight forward, and then tweets

03:08:25.600 --> 03:08:31.000
will need an ID, some text, and now is the tricky part.

03:08:31.000 --> 03:08:35.080
We actually need to model the relationship between tweets and users.

03:08:35.080 --> 03:08:39.760
So keep in mind, a user can have zero or many tweets.

03:08:39.760 --> 03:08:48.800
And this is the case, the easiest thing to do is to put a foreign key ID on the entity

03:08:48.800 --> 03:08:49.800
that is the many.

03:08:49.800 --> 03:08:52.600
So in this case, the tweets should reference the users.

03:08:52.600 --> 03:08:57.520
So tweets need a user ID.

03:08:57.520 --> 03:09:00.080
If you think about it, it starts to make sense, right?

03:09:00.080 --> 03:09:02.080
In fact, let's just jump right into some examples.

03:09:02.080 --> 03:09:10.600
Let's say that we have a user number one, name Lane, and user number two, ID two, name

03:09:10.600 --> 03:09:13.080
Alan.

03:09:13.080 --> 03:09:18.280
And then a new tweet is created ID one says hello.

03:09:18.280 --> 03:09:19.900
And let's say Lane tweeted it.

03:09:19.900 --> 03:09:27.280
So it will be user ID one, right, it references Lane over here in the users table.

03:09:27.280 --> 03:09:34.320
The next tweet will of course be ID two, because we need all of the IDs in the tweets table

03:09:34.320 --> 03:09:35.320
to be unique, right?

03:09:35.320 --> 03:09:36.640
They're the primary key.

03:09:36.640 --> 03:09:42.080
But the user ID on this one, it's hey, might be user ID two, right?

03:09:42.080 --> 03:09:44.000
So Alan tweeted this tweet.

03:09:44.000 --> 03:09:52.400
Now when we add a third tweet, yo, let's say Lane tweeted this one again, we could just

03:09:52.400 --> 03:09:54.120
reference Lane again, right?

03:09:54.120 --> 03:09:59.520
So we can have many tweets, right, in this case, tweet number one and tweet number three

03:09:59.520 --> 03:10:03.120
that are related to one user, right?

03:10:03.120 --> 03:10:05.240
User number one, which is Lane in this case.

03:10:05.240 --> 03:10:10.280
So one to many relationships, foreign key on the table of the many.

03:10:10.280 --> 03:10:13.080
Now let's talk about many to many.

03:10:13.080 --> 03:10:21.240
This is the arguably the most, the most complex relationship type, many to many users and

03:10:21.240 --> 03:10:22.480
tweets don't really make sense.

03:10:22.480 --> 03:10:27.680
So let's call, let's change out the tweets table for something else.

03:10:27.680 --> 03:10:28.680
Let's do groups.

03:10:28.680 --> 03:10:34.000
So let's say that users can organize themselves within groups.

03:10:34.000 --> 03:10:36.320
And if you think about it, this starts to make sense, right?

03:10:36.320 --> 03:10:42.360
A user should probably be able to be a part of many different groups, but a group naturally

03:10:42.360 --> 03:10:44.520
can have many different users in it.

03:10:44.520 --> 03:10:46.280
So we've got a many to many relationship.

03:10:46.280 --> 03:10:51.520
Okay, let me move these apart just a little bit.

03:10:51.520 --> 03:11:04.880
Now, a group, a group is going to have slightly different data.

03:11:04.880 --> 03:11:09.000
A group should probably have a name, I guess that makes sense.

03:11:09.000 --> 03:11:14.480
But just to keep it a little unique, let's say that a group also has a size, like a max,

03:11:14.480 --> 03:11:21.280
a max size, okay.

03:11:21.280 --> 03:11:22.840
And let's add a couple of groups.

03:11:22.840 --> 03:11:29.560
So we can still use Lane and Allen, but we'll create a new group, all the Gophers, and it

