03:30:41.920 --> 03:30:50.960
dependent on the entire primary E, not just a part of the primary key.

03:30:50.960 --> 03:30:52.080
That's kind of word soup.

03:30:52.080 --> 03:30:54.920
So let's look at an example.

03:30:54.920 --> 03:31:02.680
We've got our database, say we have three columns, we've got first name, and do last

03:31:02.680 --> 03:31:04.360
name and first initial.

03:31:04.360 --> 03:31:11.880
So first name, last name, and first initial.

03:31:11.880 --> 03:31:15.040
And then let's populate the database with some names.

03:31:15.040 --> 03:31:29.640
So we've got, say we've got two lanes, we've got a lane small, we've got a lane Brewer.

03:31:29.640 --> 03:31:37.640
And then let's say that we've got an Allen small, and then the first initial for all

03:31:37.640 --> 03:31:51.320
of these would be L, L, and A. Now this table adheres to first normal form because there's

03:31:51.320 --> 03:31:55.440
no duplicate rows and no nested tables.

03:31:55.440 --> 03:31:58.920
It does not, however, adhere to second normal form.

03:31:58.920 --> 03:32:02.240
And in order to understand why, we have to understand what we mean by primary key.

03:32:02.240 --> 03:32:07.400
So typically when you're working in a production database, the primary key in a practical

03:32:07.400 --> 03:32:11.860
sense will just be an ID column, and that ID column will always have a unique ID for

03:32:11.860 --> 03:32:13.280
every row in the table.

03:32:13.280 --> 03:32:16.720
That makes database normalization fairly easy, right?

03:32:16.720 --> 03:32:20.780
But when we're trying to understand database normalization in more of an academic sense,

03:32:20.780 --> 03:32:26.800
when we say primary key, what we mean is just kind of the smallest number of columns that

03:32:26.800 --> 03:32:29.280
we can use to uniquely identify a row.

03:32:29.280 --> 03:32:34.920
So in this case, first name can't be a primary key on its own because there's two lanes.

03:32:34.920 --> 03:32:38.560
First name can't be a primary key on its own because there's two smalls.

03:32:38.560 --> 03:32:41.980
And first initial can't be a primary key because there's two Ls.

03:32:41.980 --> 03:32:47.800
But if we say combine first and last name columns together, they are unique together,

03:32:47.800 --> 03:32:48.800
right?

03:32:48.800 --> 03:32:51.080
There's only one lane small combination.

03:32:51.080 --> 03:32:55.480
There's only one lane brewer combination, and there's only one Allen small.

03:32:55.480 --> 03:32:59.940
So our primary key is going to be the combination of first and last name columns.

03:32:59.940 --> 03:33:02.720
So with that definition in mind, let's read the rule again.

03:33:02.720 --> 03:33:07.280
All columns that are not part of the primary key, so in this case, first initial, must

03:33:07.280 --> 03:33:12.800
only be dependent on the entire primary key, not just a part of it, right?

03:33:12.800 --> 03:33:14.160
Here's the problem.

03:33:14.160 --> 03:33:19.240
First initial is entirely dependent on just the first name column.

03:33:19.240 --> 03:33:25.040
In other words, it's dependent on part of the primary key, not the entire primary key.

03:33:25.040 --> 03:33:33.480
One way to solve this problem would be to create a new table and to move, to move first

03:33:33.480 --> 03:33:42.400
initial, the first initial column down here into that second table.

03:33:42.400 --> 03:33:48.600
And then we would map first name to first initial.

03:33:48.600 --> 03:33:51.560
And now you'll see we actually have some redundancy down here.

03:33:51.560 --> 03:33:56.400
Notice that lane being mapped to L only needs to happen once, right?

03:33:56.400 --> 03:34:00.760
So we could actually remove a row.

03:34:00.760 --> 03:34:02.320
Cool.

03:34:02.320 --> 03:34:07.140
Now we have one table with unique data, and anytime we need a first initial, we can look

03:34:07.140 --> 03:34:11.880
up the first initial for a given name in a separate table.

03:34:11.880 --> 03:34:13.680
This reduces redundancy, right?

03:34:13.680 --> 03:34:18.200
We talked about how normalization reduces data duplication and data redundancy.

03:34:18.200 --> 03:34:20.720
We now have less data duplication.

03:34:20.720 --> 03:34:22.440
Moving on, we've got third normal form.

03:34:22.440 --> 03:34:27.200
So remember, the database in third normal form follows all the rules of first and second

03:34:27.200 --> 03:34:29.360
normal form, but also one additional rule.

03:34:29.360 --> 03:34:39.760
And that is that all the columns not in the primary key are dependent only on the primary

03:34:39.760 --> 03:34:41.240
key.

03:34:41.240 --> 03:34:42.840
Pendant.

03:34:42.840 --> 03:34:44.240
Let's bust out an example.

03:34:44.240 --> 03:34:46.320
I'll use the same examples last time.

03:34:46.320 --> 03:34:52.760
Let's say that we've got lane small, actually, let's do this.

03:34:52.760 --> 03:35:07.240
Let's do first name, do last name, we'll do first initial.

03:35:07.240 --> 03:35:16.120
The key difference is that this time we'll add at the start an ID column.

03:35:16.120 --> 03:35:22.540
And we'll even be good database citizens and give some unique IDs.

03:35:22.540 --> 03:35:31.240
So in this example, the primary key is literally just the ID column.

03:35:31.240 --> 03:35:32.320
And then let's add some data.

03:35:32.320 --> 03:35:41.320
So we've got, we need two lanes, lane, lane, do Allen again.

03:35:41.320 --> 03:35:52.160
And we'll do small, small, and we'll do Brewer in the middle.

03:35:52.160 --> 03:36:07.560
And then here, we've got L, L, and a, whoo, okay, now notice this table actually does

03:36:07.560 --> 03:36:12.560
adhere to second normal form technically, right?

03:36:12.560 --> 03:36:19.880
Because yes, first initial is completely dependent on first name, but first name is now not part

03:36:19.880 --> 03:36:21.560
of the primary key.

03:36:21.560 --> 03:36:24.920
So it does adhere to second normal form.

03:36:24.920 --> 03:36:29.080
I'd argue that it's a pretty stupid academic distinction.

03:36:29.080 --> 03:36:32.580
Really we have the same problem, we're duplicating data.

03:36:32.580 --> 03:36:36.280
So anyways, the way we fix it is essentially identical.

03:36:36.280 --> 03:36:44.480
We can take this table, create a new one, and strip it down to just the parts we care

03:36:44.480 --> 03:36:45.480
about.

03:36:45.480 --> 03:36:56.880
In this case, we don't need last name or ID, we would just map first names to first initials.

03:36:56.880 --> 03:37:01.320
And the way we're going to do that is by shrinking it down here.

03:37:01.320 --> 03:37:02.680
We don't need two lanes.

03:37:02.680 --> 03:37:10.000
We just need a lane and an Allen, and move this column as well, again, remove the redundancy.

03:37:10.000 --> 03:37:11.520
Cool.

03:37:11.520 --> 03:37:14.360
Now we have all the same data being represented.

03:37:14.360 --> 03:37:16.800
Why can't I move that?

03:37:16.800 --> 03:37:18.200
There we go.

03:37:18.200 --> 03:37:21.960
Anytime we need the first initial for a first name, we can look it up in this secondary

03:37:21.960 --> 03:37:23.320
table, right?

03:37:23.320 --> 03:37:28.360
We've reduced some redundancy, and we have a primary ID column.

03:37:28.360 --> 03:37:34.080
Again, the distinction between second and third normal forms in practice as a backend

03:37:34.080 --> 03:37:39.360
developer working on databases is fairly meaningless.

03:37:39.360 --> 03:37:41.760
The idea is basically the same.

03:37:41.760 --> 03:37:49.000
So voice cod normal form adds one additional rule, and that rule is that a column that

03:37:49.000 --> 03:38:05.840
is part of primary key may not be dependent on a column that is not part of the primary

03:38:05.840 --> 03:38:06.840
key.

03:38:06.840 --> 03:38:12.520
A fun historical fact is that voice cod normal form was actually created later, after the

03:38:12.520 --> 03:38:17.600
first second and third normal forms, only after realizing that there is a way for duplicate

03:38:17.600 --> 03:38:24.320
data to slip into tables, even when a table adheres to all the rules of third normal form,

03:38:24.320 --> 03:38:26.120
so this additional rule was added.

03:38:26.120 --> 03:38:30.960
Now in order for voice cod normal form to actually matter, the database actually has

03:38:30.960 --> 03:38:33.880
to be in kind of a weird and specific state.

03:38:33.880 --> 03:38:37.840
That's why it wasn't really added until later, when people actually found out that this is

03:38:37.840 --> 03:38:39.280
potentially a problem.

03:38:39.280 --> 03:38:40.880
Okay, so what do I mean?

03:38:40.880 --> 03:38:46.280
Let's take a look at this database we have here, with the four columns, release year,

03:38:46.280 --> 03:38:49.600
release date, sales, and name.

03:38:49.600 --> 03:38:56.800
The problem here is that we have multiple possible primary keys, or candidate primary

03:38:56.800 --> 03:38:57.840
keys.

03:38:57.840 --> 03:39:01.760
For example, we could just have the primary key be the name column, because all of these

03:39:01.760 --> 03:39:02.760
names are unique.

03:39:02.760 --> 03:39:06.240
Kiss me tender, bloody mary, I want to be them, he got me.

03:39:06.240 --> 03:39:07.640
They're all unique.

03:39:07.640 --> 03:39:13.040
Additionally, a possible primary key could actually be the combination of the release

03:39:13.040 --> 03:39:17.640
year column and the sales column.

03:39:17.640 --> 03:39:24.080
If you take a look, 2001, 100, 2001, 200, 2002, 100, 2002, 200, those are all actually

03:39:24.080 --> 03:39:30.080
unique combinations, so that could be a possible candidate key.

03:39:30.080 --> 03:39:36.800
Additionally, just the release date column could be its own possible candidate key.

03:39:36.800 --> 03:39:43.520
Again, the thing that sets up this problem for voice cod normal form is we have multiple

03:39:43.520 --> 03:39:45.840
possible primary keys.

03:39:45.840 --> 03:39:51.840
Now, let's say for the sake of example that we've chosen the combination of release year

03:39:51.840 --> 03:39:57.960
and sales, these two columns, release year and sales, as our primary key.

03:39:57.960 --> 03:40:05.240
Now, the problem is that release year, this column, is fully dependent on the release

03:40:05.240 --> 03:40:07.400
date column.

03:40:07.400 --> 03:40:15.000
We have a column in the primary key that is dependent on release date, which is a column

03:40:15.000 --> 03:40:18.520
that is not part of the primary key.

03:40:18.520 --> 03:40:21.400
We're breaking voice cod normal form.

03:40:21.400 --> 03:40:25.560
If you go back and read the text from second and third normal forms, you'll actually realize

03:40:25.560 --> 03:40:32.520
that this isn't strictly forbidden in those definitions of normal forms.

03:40:32.520 --> 03:40:33.520
We can fix this.

03:40:33.520 --> 03:40:43.000
The way we fix this is just by making release date not dependent on release year.

03:40:43.000 --> 03:40:50.600
We'll just do release date and we'll just store the month and the day in that column.

03:40:50.600 --> 03:41:01.480
If we were saying we might change this to say release month day or something like that,

03:41:01.480 --> 03:41:02.680
you get the idea.

03:41:02.680 --> 03:41:08.360
It's worth pointing out that the distinction between second, third, and voice cod normal

03:41:08.360 --> 03:41:11.920
forms is mostly an academic exercise.

03:41:11.920 --> 03:41:16.860
In your day to day job as a developer, you shouldn't be worried too much about the differences

03:41:16.860 --> 03:41:18.920
between all of the different normal forms.

03:41:18.920 --> 03:41:23.480
What you should be worried about is are you duplicating data?

03:41:23.480 --> 03:41:28.640
Is there a way I can restructure my tables so that when I update something in one place,

03:41:28.640 --> 03:41:31.600
I don't also need to update it in another.

03:41:31.600 --> 03:41:37.400
That will keep your database less duplicated and it will also keep it more correct because

03:41:37.400 --> 03:41:42.640
there's less of a chance of you forgetting to update multiple copies of the same data.

03:41:42.640 --> 03:41:49.200
To recap, really the whole purpose of database normalization is to get data integrity and

03:41:49.200 --> 03:41:52.320
to reduce data redundancy.

03:41:52.320 --> 03:41:59.440
One great example of data integrity is I like to think of a user's age and their birthday.

03:41:59.440 --> 03:42:07.180
If we store their age in a database, like age equals six, that data becomes incorrect

03:42:07.180 --> 03:42:08.600
with the passage of time.

03:42:08.600 --> 03:42:12.960
If we don't touch that data next year, it will just be wrong.

03:42:12.960 --> 03:42:16.360
It will just be out of date.

03:42:16.360 --> 03:42:18.360
That data does not have integrity.

03:42:18.360 --> 03:42:19.760
It becomes incorrect.

03:42:19.760 --> 03:42:20.760
That's a problem.

03:42:20.760 --> 03:42:26.200
Anytime it's easy for your data to become incorrect or it becomes incorrect with time,

03:42:26.200 --> 03:42:28.420
you don't have good data integrity.

03:42:28.420 --> 03:42:34.200
If instead you store the birthday, now that never gets old.

03:42:34.200 --> 03:42:38.400
We can always calculate an age from a birthday when we need it.

03:42:38.400 --> 03:42:42.920
Similarly, data redundancy, obviously we've talked about this, but we don't want multiple

03:42:42.920 --> 03:42:45.840
copies of the same data in a database.

03:42:45.840 --> 03:42:50.800
That can actually lead to data integrity problems because maybe we update it in one spot but

03:42:50.800 --> 03:42:52.360
forget to update it in another.

03:42:52.360 --> 03:42:54.520
Now we have incorrect, mismatching data.

03:42:54.520 --> 03:42:59.560
The question is, to improve data integrity, data should generally be stored in a blank

03:42:59.560 --> 03:43:04.640
form, precalculated or raw.

03:43:04.640 --> 03:43:11.560
Now if we precalculate the data, the problem is again, it can become out of date if the

03:43:11.560 --> 03:43:13.700
underlying raw data changes.

03:43:13.700 --> 03:43:18.380
So I would argue store the raw data makes it easier to update.

03:43:18.380 --> 03:43:21.040
You can always calculate the result later.

03:43:21.040 --> 03:43:27.040
The only time you ever really want to store precalculated data is for very specific performance

03:43:27.040 --> 03:43:28.280
reasons, right?

03:43:28.280 --> 03:43:32.320
And like all things with performance, it's not the first thing you should optimize.

03:43:32.320 --> 03:43:35.800
You really would only start to look into that if your queries were getting really slow and

03:43:35.800 --> 03:43:39.520
becoming a problem.

03:43:39.520 --> 03:43:42.560
Pick the best example of data redundancy.

03:43:42.560 --> 03:43:46.160
Each table has an ID field as its primary key.

03:43:46.160 --> 03:43:47.720
It's not redundant.

03:43:47.720 --> 03:43:51.160
A user's address is stored in two different tables.

03:43:51.160 --> 03:43:53.640
Yeah, that seems like a problem, right?

03:43:53.640 --> 03:43:56.320
Now when they update their address, we're going to have to update it in multiple places.

03:43:56.320 --> 03:43:58.080
By the way, this is like what governments do.

03:43:58.080 --> 03:44:01.560
I don't know if you've ever been through this where like you update your address with the

03:44:01.560 --> 03:44:06.080
DMV and like they update it, but then like it's not updated on your taxes.

03:44:06.080 --> 03:44:10.840
It's because like every government agency has their own database and so it's not all

03:44:10.840 --> 03:44:11.840
synced up.

03:44:11.840 --> 03:44:12.840
It's not all in sync.

03:44:12.840 --> 03:44:16.020
This is the whole problem of data redundancy, right?

03:44:16.020 --> 03:44:18.800
So you don't want that within your own system.

03:44:18.800 --> 03:44:21.920
I'm just going to read these other ones just in case.

03:44:21.920 --> 03:44:25.560
Two tables can both contain fields that store Booleans.

03:44:25.560 --> 03:44:30.000
Not a problem because as long as they're different columns that mean different things, that's

03:44:30.000 --> 03:44:31.360
not necessarily a problem.

03:44:31.360 --> 03:44:35.120
A country table and a user's table each contain a field called name.

03:44:35.120 --> 03:44:37.320
Again, not really a problem.

03:44:37.320 --> 03:44:41.340
And frankly, the more you work with production databases, the more you'll kind of find very

03:44:41.340 --> 03:44:44.640
common patterns amongst the names of columns.

03:44:44.640 --> 03:44:46.680
Almost every table will have an ID.

03:44:46.680 --> 03:44:49.480
Most tables will have a name column.

03:44:49.480 --> 03:44:53.620
Many tables will have a created at column, right, so that you can see when each individual

