03:52:29.600 --> 03:52:36.200
This next question is just, which should you optimize for first, generally speaking, reducing

03:52:36.200 --> 03:52:38.240
duplicate data or speed?

03:52:38.240 --> 03:52:43.600
Now this is really just kind of a rule of thumb in software engineering.

03:52:43.600 --> 03:52:46.880
This isn't like a hard and fast fact.

03:52:46.880 --> 03:52:53.240
But in my opinion, and I hold this opinion fairly strongly, generally speaking, you should

03:52:53.240 --> 03:52:58.600
start with just reducing duplicate data because you're going to have fewer bugs in your application

03:52:58.600 --> 03:52:59.600
that way.

03:52:59.600 --> 03:53:01.000
You'll be able to move faster as a team.

03:53:01.000 --> 03:53:05.120
You'll be able to fix bugs, ship features, refactor code, right?

03:53:05.120 --> 03:53:07.800
Do all of the things that you normally have to do as a developer.

03:53:07.800 --> 03:53:13.960
You'll be able to do them better if you don't have to deal with duplicate data, right?

03:53:13.960 --> 03:53:21.900
If you have speed problems that can easily be solved or are best solved by introducing

03:53:21.900 --> 03:53:24.660
some duplicate data, that's okay.

03:53:24.660 --> 03:53:28.320
It's not like you can never, ever, ever have duplicate data, right?

03:53:28.320 --> 03:53:33.280
I mean, at the end of the day, caching just is duplicate data, right?

03:53:33.280 --> 03:53:37.620
It's not necessarily a problem, but cache invalidation is hard, right?

03:53:37.620 --> 03:53:44.040
So we kind of stay away from it as long as we can.

