03:54:12.280 --> 03:54:14.640
This isn't, again, this isn't a hard and fast rule.

03:54:14.640 --> 03:54:18.680
It's not like your application is going to break if you name the column key or identifier

03:54:18.680 --> 03:54:25.200
or skeleton key, but like everyone uses ID, just freaking use it.

03:54:25.200 --> 03:54:28.480
Like, why don't be confusing, right?

03:54:28.480 --> 03:54:32.800
It's kind of like how everyone uses I in loops, right?

03:54:32.800 --> 03:54:40.240
Or I less than zero, like there's just a convention around some things in programming.

03:54:40.240 --> 03:54:43.160
And if you stick to them, it'll make your life a little bit easier.

03:54:43.160 --> 03:54:47.600
This question is which is more important for your career in backend development, memorizing

03:54:47.600 --> 03:54:53.700
the exact definitions of first, second, third, and Boyce Cod normal form, or internalizing

03:54:53.700 --> 03:54:58.840
simple rules of thumb regarding database normalization?

03:54:58.840 --> 03:55:02.640
The answer is internalizing the simple rules of thumb.

03:55:02.640 --> 03:55:06.480
That's going to make you a much better backend engineer, in my opinion, right?

03:55:06.480 --> 03:55:10.520
You do not need to know the exact definitions of these, of these normal forms.

03:55:10.520 --> 03:55:15.780
They are useful theoretical exercises for us to think about normalization, right?

03:55:15.780 --> 03:55:18.420
But these rules of thumb, right?

03:55:18.420 --> 03:55:25.160
Things like adding primary keys to all of our rows, avoiding duplicate data, not storing

03:55:25.160 --> 03:55:28.800
data that's completely dependent on other data, right?

03:55:28.800 --> 03:55:33.640
Having our schema simple, those things are what you're going to really use day to day

03:55:33.640 --> 03:55:36.120
and what you need to keep top of mind.

03:55:36.120 --> 03:55:44.480
