03:55:49.680 --> 03:55:53.520
Now joins are actually one of the most powerful features of relational databases.

03:55:53.520 --> 03:55:58.200
In fact, without joins, it almost wouldn't even make sense to worry about all these different

03:55:58.200 --> 03:56:01.040
complex relationships between tables.

03:56:01.040 --> 03:56:07.000
To put it as simply as I can, a join is just a way to query multiple tables in an SQL database

03:56:07.000 --> 03:56:08.280
at the same time.

03:56:08.280 --> 03:56:12.440
Now the first type of join that we're going to talk about is called an inner join.

03:56:12.440 --> 03:56:16.640
It's also kind of the simplest and the most common type of join.

03:56:16.640 --> 03:56:19.520
So by default, a join command is an inner join.

03:56:19.520 --> 03:56:25.560
So if you just use the join keyword in an SQL statement, you'll be doing an inner join.

03:56:25.560 --> 03:56:31.120
And an inner join returns all of the records in table A that have a matching record in

03:56:31.120 --> 03:56:33.000
table B.

03:56:33.000 --> 03:56:35.280
So we have two tables, right?

03:56:35.280 --> 03:56:40.480
And we'll get to the syntax of a join here in just a second, but understand that what

03:56:40.480 --> 03:56:45.240
we're talking about is we've got some number of rows in table A and we've got some number

03:56:45.240 --> 03:56:49.580
of rows in table B. Let's say we have 100 rows in each.

03:56:49.580 --> 03:56:55.920
When we write our join query, we're trying to get a single result set back, right?

03:56:55.920 --> 03:57:01.040
So if we got all of those rows back, we'd get 200 rows, right?

03:57:01.040 --> 03:57:08.120
Well an inner join is just saying, I don't want necessarily all of the rows.

03:57:08.120 --> 03:57:12.480
I want all of the rows that exist in both tables, right?

03:57:12.480 --> 03:57:15.520
And we typically link on some sort of ID.

03:57:15.520 --> 03:57:20.240
So you can see by this diagram, essentially what we're saying with an inner join is I

03:57:20.240 --> 03:57:26.240
just want the set of rows from both tables that exist in both tables, right?

03:57:26.240 --> 03:57:34.240
So let's say that in table A we have 100 records and 25 of them share some ID, some linking

03:57:34.240 --> 03:57:41.880
key with table B. It's only those rows that we're going to return in the result set.

03:57:41.880 --> 03:57:44.580
Let's take a look at an actual example.

